{
  "concept": "BUILD-MAX-HEAP turns an arbitrary array into a max-heap in place by calling MAX-HEAPIFY on every internal node from index ⌊n/2⌋ down to 1, so that each sift-down operates on subtrees whose children are already valid heaps.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal array bar labeled 'A' with ten cells holding 4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7, each cell outlined and light gray",
    "Index tick labels 1..10 beneath (or above) the array cells linking array position to tree index",
    "Binary tree drawing of the 10-element heap: root at top center, levels at depth 0-3",
    "Ten circular nodes, each showing its key value inside the circle in large numerals",
    "Small index numeral (1..10) drawn just outside and above-left/right of each node circle",
    "Straight edges connecting parent node i to children 2i and 2i+1",
    "Dark gray fill on the single node currently being processed, annotated with an italic 'i' label beside it",
    "Light gray fill on all other nodes",
    "Highlight/outline on the two children being compared during a MAX-HEAPIFY step",
    "Comparison arrows from node i down to its left and right children during a comparison step",
    "Swap indicator: curved double-headed arrow between the two nodes whose values are exchanged",
    "Subtree shading marking the already-heapified region as the loop progresses",
    "Panel caption/status line naming the current stage (e.g. 'i = 5', 'MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 5)', 'done')",
    "Legend: dark gray = current node i, light gray = untouched, outline = compared children",
    "Step counter / operation log showing the sequence of compare and swap calls",
    "Success banner stating 'every parent ≥ both children'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "heapifyEditor",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That MAX-HEAPIFY is a sift-down: compare the node with both children, pick the largest, and if it is not the node itself, swap and recurse — and that BUILD-MAX-HEAP works only because this is applied bottom-up, so each call's children are already heaps.",
      "label": "Implement maxHeapify(i, heapSize)",
      "entry_point": "maxHeapify(i, heapSize)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "value(i)", "does": "read the key stored at 1-based index i" },
        { "op": "left(i)", "does": "return 2*i, the left child index" },
        { "op": "right(i)", "does": "return 2*i + 1, the right child index" },
        { "op": "greater(i, j)", "does": "true when value(i) > value(j); records a comparison, drawing arrows from i to j in the tree" },
        { "op": "swap(i, j)", "does": "exchange the keys at i and j — the ONLY way to mutate the heap" },
        { "op": "mark(i, text)", "does": "shade node i dark gray and label it with your own note (e.g. 'i' or 'largest') for this frame" }
      ],
      "frame": "the full array bar plus the whole tree, with the current index i shaded dark gray, the 1-2 child indices touched by the op outlined, and an arrow or swap marker on the pair involved",
      "input": { "label": "Initial array A", "default": "4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7" },
      "sample_code": "function maxHeapify(i, heapSize) {\n  mark(i, 'i');\n  const l = left(i);\n  const r = right(i);\n  let largest = i;\n  if (l <= heapSize && greater(l, largest)) largest = l;\n  if (r <= heapSize && greater(r, largest)) largest = r;\n  if (largest !== i) {\n    swap(i, largest);\n    maxHeapify(largest, heapSize);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function maxHeapify(i, heapSize) {\n  mark(i, 'i');\n  const l = left(i);\n  const r = right(i);\n  let largest = i;\n  if (l <= heapSize && greater(l, largest)) largest = l;\n  if (r <= heapSize && greater(r, largest)) largest = r;\n  if (largest !== i) {\n    swap(i, largest);\n    // BUG: no recursive call — the demoted key stops sinking after one level,\n    // so the root 4 lands at index 2 and never reaches its correct place.\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "after BUILD-MAX-HEAP runs, every index i in 1..n/2 satisfies value(i) >= value(2i) and value(i) >= value(2i+1); the final tree matches panel (f) with root 16"
    },
    {
      "id": "buildRun",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "That BUILD-MAX-HEAP is just a loop calling the learner's MAX-HEAPIFY on i = ⌊n/2⌋ down to 1, and that the six textbook panels are exactly the snapshots taken at the start of each of those calls.",
      "label": "Run BUILD-MAX-HEAP",
      "states": ["idle", "running", "done"],
      "default": "idle"
    },
    {
      "id": "panelStep",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That the heap becomes valid from the bottom up: at step i, every subtree rooted below i is already a max-heap, which is precisely the precondition MAX-HEAPIFY needs.",
      "label": "Loop index i (panel a→f)",
      "range": [5, 0, 1],
      "default": 5
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Panel (a): start at i = 5",
      "narration": "The array A = ⟨4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7⟩ is drawn as a tree. Leaves 6..10 are already trivial heaps, so the first real work is at index 5 (value 16), shaded dark gray.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 5, "buildRun": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Write MAX-HEAPIFY",
      "narration": "In the editor, compare node i with its left and right children, keep the largest, and if it isn't i, swap and recurse. Every compare and swap you call becomes one animation frame in the tree.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 5, "buildRun": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (b): i = 4",
      "narration": "MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 5) found 16 already larger than its only child 7, so nothing moved. The loop steps to i = 4 (value 2), whose children 14 and 8 both exceed it.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 4, "buildRun": "running" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panels (c)–(d): i = 3, then i = 2",
      "narration": "2 sinks below 14, then 3 swaps with 10, then 1 swaps with 16 and keeps sinking. Each call only has to fix the top node because everything beneath it is already a valid heap.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 2, "buildRun": "running" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (e): i = 1, the root sifts down",
      "narration": "The last call starts at the root: 4 must travel two levels, swapping with 16 and then with 7, before the whole tree satisfies the heap property.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 1, "buildRun": "running" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (f): a max-heap",
      "narration": "No node is shaded; root 16 is the maximum and every parent dominates its children. Try the buggy version that skips the recursive call and watch exactly where the tree diverges.",
      "state": { "panelStep": 0, "buildRun": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "MAX-HEAPIFY can only fix a node whose two subtrees are already heaps — so BUILD-MAX-HEAP runs the loop backwards from ⌊n/2⌋ to 1, guaranteeing that precondition for free at every call.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single active panel replacing the textbook's 6-panel grid: array bar 'A' with ten outlined light-gray cells across the top (indices labeled 1..10), and below it a centered rooted binary tree with 10 circled nodes — root at index 1, level 2 at indices 2-3, level 3 at 4-7, level 4 at 8-10 (children of 4 are 8 and 9; left child of 5 is 10). Nodes are light gray circles with a black key inside and a small index numeral just outside; edges are thin straight black lines. The currently processed node is filled dark gray with an italic 'i' label beside it, matching the textbook. During a comparison frame, draw thin arrows from i to the compared children and outline those children; during a swap, show a curved double-headed arrow and animate the two values exchanging in both the tree and the array bar simultaneously so the array↔tree correspondence is obvious. A caption under the tree reads 'MAX-HEAPIFY(A, i)' with the current i, and a small op log lists the compare/swap calls the learner's code issued. Final state shows root 16 / children 14 and 10 with no dark node and a green 'valid max-heap' banner; on failure, highlight in red the first parent-child pair that violates the heap property.",
  "_note": "panelStep slider range [5,0,1] steps downward from i=5 to 0 (0 = finished panel f); treat the third value as step magnitude."
}

