        "buildRun": "idle"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (b): i = 4",
      "narration": "MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 5) found 16 already larger than its only child 7, so nothing moved. The loop steps to i = 4 (value 2), whose children 14 and 8 both exceed it.",
      "state": {
        "panelStep": 4,
        "buildRun": "running"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panels (c)–(d): i = 3, then i = 2",
      "narration": "2 sinks below 14, then 3 swaps with 10, then 1 swaps with 16 and keeps sinking. Each call only has to fix the top node because everything beneath it is already a valid heap.",
      "state": {
        "panelStep": 2,
        "buildRun": "running"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (e): i = 1, the root sifts down",
      "narration": "The last call starts at the root: 4 must travel two levels, swapping with 16 and then with 7, before the whole tree satisfies the heap property.",
      "state": {
        "panelStep": 1,
        "buildRun": "running"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Panel (f): a max-heap",
      "narration": "No node is shaded; root 16 is the maximum and every parent dominates its children. Try the buggy version that skips the recursive call and watch exactly where the tree diverges.",
      "state": {
        "panelStep": 0,
        "buildRun": "done"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "MAX-HEAPIFY can only fix a node whose two subtrees are already heaps — so BUILD-MAX-HEAP runs the loop backwards from ⌊n/2⌋ to 1, guaranteeing that precondition for free at every call.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single active panel replacing the textbook's 6-panel grid: array bar 'A' with ten outlined light-gray cells across the top (indices labeled 1..10), and below it a centered rooted binary tree with 10 circled nodes — root at index 1, level 2 at indices 2-3, level 3 at 4-7, level 4 at 8-10 (children of 4 are 8 and 9; left child of 5 is 10). Nodes are light gray circles with a black key inside and a small index numeral just outside; edges are thin straight black lines. The currently processed node is filled dark gray with an italic 'i' label beside it, matching the textbook. During a comparison frame, draw thin arrows from i to the compared children and outline those children; during a swap, show a curved double-headed arrow and animate the two values exchanging in both the tree and the array bar simultaneously so the array↔tree correspondence is obvious. A caption under the tree reads 'MAX-HEAPIFY(A, i)' with the current i, and a small op log lists the compare/swap calls the learner's code issued. Final state shows root 16 / children 14 and 10 with no dark node and a green 'valid max-heap' banner; on failure, highlight in red the first parent-child pair that violates the heap property.",
  "_note": "panelStep slider range [5,0,1] steps downward from i=5 to 0 (0 = finished panel f); treat the third value as step magnitude."
}

